-- lib/x86.cub — the code fragment to x86-64 bytes (PLAN.md revision 8, 9 Nov 2026, git 91167ad638c0:PLAN.md, §5.3 “The target is a data type with a meaning”, §5.4 “The reader, and why the path is by construction” and §5.5 “Bytes, the boundary, and what is trusted”). Loaded after lib/machine.cub.
-- There is no intermediate syntax: the program a code compiles to is the code's normal form in the
-- fragment (lib/machine.cub), and encode prints each construct of the fragment by a fixed template.
-- Only specialization is checked: its result converts with the definition or carries a path
-- (jit-plan's ok). Everything after it (the passes x-push, x-lift, x-erase, the templates, the
-- layout, the value representation and the calling convention) is trusted: none carries a relation
-- to the specialized code, and its output is bound and run, so a wrong pass can answer wrongly. This
-- translator is to be replaced by realization through C whose type carries that relation
-- (docs/Hyperactive_Final_Paper.pdf, 11 Nov 2026, sha256 e8f358b4cd2c…, §9.3 “An exact decoded machine and a structural compiler theorem”, "Loading, native substitution, and export").
--
-- Values in compiled code, uniformly 64 bits, so no pass needs a type:
--   a word, a primitive value (Int64, UInt32, UInt64, Float64 by its bits): the bits;
--   a constructor with no fields: its index (below 4096, never an address);
--   a constructor with fields: the address of an arena cell [index][field]…[field], each field 64 bits;
--   a pair: a cell [0][first][second];
--   bytes: the address of [length][bytes, padded to 8];
--   a Nat: the count of its successors as a word (the encoder's choice, trusted with the
--   templates until the template equations of Phase 2): zero is 0, suc k is k + 1 (past 2^64
--   the overflow exit, and the net computes the call), a case on a Nat tests for 0 and binds the
--   predecessor. So an offset or a length, which the machine takes as a Word or a Nat alike, is a
--   word whichever it was.
-- Booleans are the constructors true (0) and false (1) of Bool. The boundary (src/lang.c, jit_in and
-- jit_read) converts between these and the net's values by the definition's type.
--
-- Registers: rax the value of the expression being computed, rcx and rdx scratch, rbp the frame,
-- r15 the arena's next free byte and r12 its end, r13 the call's context (word 0 the overflow flag),
-- r14 the blob's base (calls are base-relative), rbx the stack pointer at entry. A Word operation
-- whose result passes 2^64, and an arena that is full, leave through the overflow exit: the flag is
-- set and the call returns at once; the boundary then computes the call on the net (run p f ≡ f).
-- Internal calls push their arguments left to right; argument i of n is at [rbp + 16 + 8(n−1−i)];
-- local slot s is at [rbp − 8(s+1)].
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- bytes
import "first-order.cub"
